# rules: csharp-credential-written-to-the-log
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-532.cs
# from: CWE-532 (Insertion of Sensitive Information into Log File)
#
# A log is the least protected copy of anything. It is read by people who are
# not allowed near the database, shipped to a third party for aggregation, kept
# long after the credential is rotated, and included in the support bundle
# somebody emails. A password that reaches one has to be treated as disclosed.
#
# The rule reports a logging call whose *arguments* name a credential. That is
# the important word. Guarding on the whole call would report
# `LogInformation("password check complete")`, where the only mention is in the
# message, and that is the single most common false positive this rule can
# have: log messages talk about passwords constantly without containing one.
# So the message argument is excluded and the values are what is read.
#
# Which leaves the case the message *is* the value - `LogDebug($"...{token}")`.
# An interpolated string carries the value inside it, so it is read too, but
# only its holes: `$"refreshing {accessToken}"` is a finding and
# `LogDebug("refreshing the token")` is not. The distinction is the braces, and
# it is the whole reason interpolation is handled separately here rather than
# folded in with the plain arguments.
#
# `WriteLine` is in the list beside the ILogger and Serilog methods. Console is
# not a logger, but in a service it goes to the same journal, and a credential
# written to stdout is as disclosed as one written through Serilog.
#
# The call is matched on the *method name* through a hole rather than on a list
# of receivers, and that is not tidiness. `Console.WriteLine($$$_)` misses
# `System.Console.WriteLine(...)`, because a literal receiver in a pattern has
# to be spelled the way the source spells it and a three-part one does not
# parse at all. `$L.$M($V, $$$_);` with a guard on `$M` matches both, and every
# logger anyone wraps their own around.
#
# Three argument positions are matched - first, second, and last - because a
# structured logging call puts the message first and the values after it, and
# the credential is as likely to be the third argument as the second. There is
# no way to say "any argument" in a pattern, so the positions are written out.

["$L.$M($V, $$$_);", "$L.$M($_, $V, $$$_);", "$L.$M($$$_, $V);",
 "$T $X = $L.$M($V, $$$_);"] as $calls
| "^(?:LogInformation|LogDebug|LogTrace|LogWarning|LogError|LogCritical|Log|Information|Debug|Warning|Error|Verbose|Fatal|WriteLine|Write)$" as $isALogCall
| "(?i)pass(word|wd)|pwd|secret|token|api_?key|credential|connection_?string|private_?key|session_?id" as $namesACredential
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $calls) as $calls_found
| ($calls_found | of($calls) | where_capture("M"; $isALogCall) | focus("V")) as $values
| ( ($values | where_capture("V"; "^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*$") | where_capture("V"; $namesACredential))
    + ($values | where_capture("V"; "^\\$\"")
       | where_capture("V"; "\\{[^}]*(?i:pass(word|wd)|pwd|secret|token|api_?key|credential|private_?key|session_?id)[^}]*\\}"))
    + ($values | where_capture("V"; "\\+") | where_capture("V"; $namesACredential)) )
| finding("csharp-credential-written-to-the-log";
    "this writes a credential to the log, which is the least protected copy of "
    + "anything: shipped off the machine, kept past the rotation, and read by "
    + "people who are not allowed near the thing it opens")
| report
